  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Wednesday: An upper level trough developing over \nBritish Columbia will swing south through the area later this \n(Tuesday) morning through the afternoon. It will bring light rain \nshowers to the Cascades, northern mountains, and northern Idaho \nPanhandle. Places such as Omak, Colville, Chelan, and Bonners Ferry \nhave a 20 percent chance and above at seeing 0.10 inches of rainfall \nfrom Tuesday through Wednesday morning. Forcing associated with the \nincoming low combined with modest instability (a couple hundred J/kg \nof CAPE) will support a 10 to 15 percent chance of thunderstorms \nacross areas receiving rain. Soundings show saturated mid and low \nlevels (RH values greater than 80 percent), and PWATs near 0.7 \ninches. This indicates that main threats with any thunderstorms will \nbe isolated heavy downpours. Elsewhere, expect dry conditions.\n\nBy Wednesday, the low will shift southward and retrograde slightly \neastward. Rising heights downstream of a ridge off the Canadian \ncoast will promote a warming and drying trend over the Inland \nNorthwest. Temperatures on Wednesday will be in the mid 70s, which \nis right around normal for this time of year. No precipitation is \nexpected. \n\nThursday through Monday: The aforementioned low will then begin \nweakening and moving northeastward. While much of the forecast area \nwill continue seeing a drying trend, the low will brush far \nsoutheast Washington and the central Idaho mountains Thursday night \ninto early Friday morning. Main areas impacted will be the Lewiston \nand Camas Prairie areas. A 10 to 20 percent chance of\nthunderstorms will accompany these light rain showers. While \nPWATs will still be high, mid and low level moisture will be \nlower than on Tuesday, and DCAPE values will be near 1000 J/kg. \nThis suggests that the main threat with these thunderstorms will\nbe gusty outflow winds. \n\nAfter this low weakens and moves completely out of the forecast \narea, high heights will dominate the weather pattern, bringing a \nwarming and drying trend through the weekend starting on Thursday. \nTemperatures will progressively rise through the weekend, with mid \nrange guidance showing high temperatures reaching the 80s by Sunday. \nAfter the brief showers Thursday into Friday, the forecast area sees \nnear zero chances of precipitation through the weekend. Cluster \nanalysis unanimously supports this warming and drying trend through \nSunday. Beyond Sunday, however, cluster analysis lowers slightly, \nthough 75 percent of clusters broadly show a low pressure system \napproaching the coast. There is little agreement on track, strength, \nand placement, but this does hint at a potential return to unsettled \nweather next week. /AS",
